- "isle" appears in 4 of the 8 steered continuations ("isle has a tarn", "isle of steel-steens", "isle of crouches", "isle of brooch")
- "has a pike" / "with a pike" appears 3 times — pike being a fish
- "tarn" (a mountain lake), "rope", "hocket" (hook-like), "plank" (a kind of bait/lure), "crouch"

The repeated garbled template is essentially "isle ... has a [water/fish thing]", i.e. island/lake/fish imagery. That's an ocean/water-world association spread across most of the output. The only other candidate signals are isolated single-fragment hits: "road" + "wheel" once (cars) and "rookie"/"knight" once (chess) — each confined to one item and heavily corrupted, unlike the isle/pike/tarn cluster that recurs across the run.

The thought shifts the steered text toward islands, lakes, and fish — i.e. the ocean.

